@page "/countdown-timer/details"

<ComponentDetail Title="Countdown Timer"
                 SubTitle="A percent progress and completion countdown abstraction">

    <RepoLinks>
        @ComponentDetail.SideLink(("CountdownTimer.cs", TagInfo.Library, "https://github.com/Rumhyek/RForge/blob/main/src/RForge/RForge.Abstractions/CountdownTimer.cs"))
        @ComponentDetail.SideLink(("TimerStatus.cs", TagInfo.Enum, "https://github.com/Rumhyek/RForge/blob/main/src/RForge/RForge.Abstractions/TimerStatus.cs"))
    </RepoLinks>

    <Purpose>
        <div class="content">

            <p>
                <code>CountdownTimer</code> (in <code>RForge.Abstractions</code>) runs a fixed duration and reports back
                while it runs: it aims for 100 ticks (one per percent), calls your <code>OnTick</code> callback with the
                percent complete on every tick, and calls your <code>OnElapsed</code> callback once when the countdown
                actually finishes. The library uses it internally to time toast auto dismissal in
                <code>RfNotificationManager</code>.
            </p>

            <h4>When to Use</h4>
            <p>
                Use <code>CountdownTimer</code> when you have a known duration and want percent progress plus one
                completion signal: driving a progress bar, an auto dismissal delay, or a job timeout.
            </p>

            <h4>When not to Use</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Wall clock precise scheduling. The ticks ride on <code>PeriodicTimer</code> continuations, so they
                    are timely, not exact.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Counting down several independent durations: create one <code>CountdownTimer</code> per countdown.
                    A single instance runs one countdown at a time.
                </li>
            </ul>

        </div>
    </Purpose>

    <BasicUsage>
        <div class="content">

            <h3>The Status values</h3>
            <p><code>CountdownTimer.Status</code> is a <code>TimerStatus</code> with exactly three values:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <code>Stopped</code>: the timer has been constructed but never ran. This is the value right after the
                    constructor and after subscribing callbacks.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <code>Running</code>: set by <code>StartAsync</code> while the countdown is in flight.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <code>Completed</code>: set only by the real completion path: the tick that carries the countdown to
                    100 percent, immediately before your <code>OnElapsed</code> callback fires.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3>The Status lifecycle</h3>
            <ol>
                <li>The constructor leaves <code>Status</code> at <code>Stopped</code>.</li>
                <li>
                    <code>OnTick</code> and <code>OnElapsed</code> only register callbacks. Subscribing completes
                    nothing and changes <code>Status</code> not at all: after constructing and subscribing,
                    <code>Status</code> is still <code>Stopped</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <code>StartAsync</code> resets the percent to 0, sets <code>Running</code>, and runs the tick loop.
                    It is awaited: it returns when the countdown finishes or the token ends the loop.
                </li>
                <li>
                    On the tick that reaches 100 percent the timer sets <code>Completed</code> and then invokes
                    <code>OnElapsed</code>. Nothing else ever sets <code>Completed</code>.
                </li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                What you can key off: an instance that was only constructed and subscribed is inert, so it is safe to
                build the timer and wire its callbacks at startup; <code>Status == TimerStatus.Completed</code> after an
                awaited <code>StartAsync</code> means the countdown ran to the end and your elapsed callback has already
                fired; and a fresh <code>StartAsync</code> on a finished timer resets the percent and runs again with
                <code>Running</code> set anew.
            </p>

            <h5>Example: percent progress with a completion signal</h5>
            <pre><code>using var timer = new CountdownTimer(2000);

timer.OnTick(percent =>
{
    ProgressBar.Value = percent;
    return Task.CompletedTask;
});

timer.OnElapsed(() => Console.WriteLine("countdown done"));

// Subscribing completes nothing: Status is still Stopped before the first run.
Console.WriteLine(timer.Status); // Stopped

await timer.StartAsync();

if (timer.Status == TimerStatus.Completed)
{
    // The countdown truly finished and the OnElapsed callback already ran.
}</code></pre>

            <div class="notification">
                <strong>NOTE:</strong> The timeout must be positive; a zero or negative value throws an
                <code>ArgumentOutOfRangeException</code> naming <code>timeout</code>. The timer aims for 100 ticks but
                never ticks faster than the operating system timer can deliver (about 15.6 ms), so a short timeout gets
                fewer ticks: <code>OnTick</code> percent values advance in larger steps, still ending at exactly 100,
                and the countdown closes within one tick of the requested time.
            </div>

            <div class="notification">
                <strong>NOTE:</strong> A cancellation token given to the constructor ends the tick loop and
                <code>StartAsync</code> returns, but <code>TimerStatus</code> has no <code>Cancelled</code> value: the
                timer still reports <code>Running</code>. Treat "the awaited <code>StartAsync</code> returned and
                <code>Status</code> is not <code>Completed</code>" as "the countdown did not finish".
                Calling <code>StartAsync</code> while a run is already in flight (for example from a double tapped
                button) is ignored and returns a completed task, so one countdown runs and <code>OnElapsed</code>
                fires exactly once. Once a run has finished, <code>StartAsync</code> starts a new countdown.
            </div>

        </div>
    </BasicUsage>

